00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.200
To start this off, let's
briefly talk about my story.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.050
What did I do?

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.160
When I was a young
student at the end of my

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.610
undergraduate Bachelor degree,

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.440
I did an internship with
McKinsey in the Munich office.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.600
And after the internship,
I received a job offer

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.450
to join McKinsey full-time
as a business analyst.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.700
And I know that this is
not specific to McKinsey,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.480
but if you look into other MBB firms,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.560
such as BCG or Bain,
similar things are possible,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.040
as is with some of the investment banks.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.010
Internships, they offer a
great, amazing opportunity,

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.380
not only to collect some work experience

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.030
and to polish your CVs,
but also to actually

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.070
receive a chance to secure a job offer

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.550
that you can directly use
then, after university studies.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.120
And I need to tell you that
I'm really proud of you guys

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.540
in the Firm Learning community.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.270
Because I did a survey
on my Instagram account,

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.550
and I asked you how many of you already

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.780
received a job offer after internship?

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.850
And indeed, more than 40% of you

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.070
told me that yes, you already received

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.570
a job offer after internship.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.430
I believe this is a very high quota,

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.790
and for sure, significantly above average.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.230
So you already know what
grade of an opportunity

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:57.642
internship can be, and
to all of those of you

00:01:57.642 --> 00:02:00.260
who haven't received such an offer yet,

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.790
please take this as an inspiration.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.480
This is possible, and I hope
that my tips can help you

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.230
to turn your consulting
internship into a job offer,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.280
and therefore into a
great success for you.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:11.620
In this video, I'm going
to give you crucial tips

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.670
on three areas, and what you can do

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:16.370
to secure your job offer
after an internship.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:18.550
And the key takeaway is that as an intern,

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.160
you're basically
evaluated against the role

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.590
of a full-time consultant.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.947
So the people you work
with will ask themselves,

00:02:24.947 --> 00:02:26.147
"If I would hire this guy,

00:02:26.147 --> 00:02:29.010
"could he be a successful
consultant today?"

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:30.820
So in order to understand
what you need to do

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:32.450
in order to receive such an offer,

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.660
you need to understand how
consultants are evaluated,

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.670
how their performance is measured.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.490
And while, of course,
every single consultancy

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.410
has its own evaluation
scheme, which differs a bit

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.410
in the language and the
terms that are being used,

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.640
in the end, it comes
down to three dimensions

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.660
that pretty much all consulting firms use

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.720
to evaluate their
consulting level employees.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.410
The first is your analytical skills.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.630
So how good are your
intellectual capabilities?

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:55.710
How good are you at Excel?

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.670
How well are you to perform an analysis,

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.220
and what do you contribute
in problem-solving sessions?

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:01.970
The second are your
skills with the client.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:03.780
So how do you interact with the client?

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.420
Does the client take you seriously?

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.230
Are you able to lead meetings?

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.990
Just to interact with the client well,

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:10.460
and be perceived by the client

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.250
as a competent business professional.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.000
And the third dimension
are your team skills.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.510
So are you a team player?

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.970
Can you interact and
cooperate with the team well?

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.300
Are you bringing value
to how the team operates,

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.720
and are you really making
a positive contribution

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.780
to solving the problems
that the team has at hand?

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:27.430
The first skill that
you need to demonstrate

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.790
as an intern are your analytical skills.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.140
In many consulting internships,

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.130
one of the first tasks that you will get

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.810
are some little analytical tasks.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:36.830
Your project lead might
give you a data set,

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.240
some Excel files, and
will ask you to conduct

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.970
a couple of analyses, maybe
to build a small model,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:43.357
that is in some way related to the work

00:03:43.357 --> 00:03:45.060
that they team is doing.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.760
Here the very first tip I have for you

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.360
is to just be extremely
diligent in your work.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.680
There are some junior colleagues,

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.740
that if you give them a certain
piece of work, you just know

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.710
that there will be some errors in it.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.660
Some junior colleagues tend to hand in

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.000
a certain piece of work,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.240
well-knowing that this is not ready yet.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.290
That the project lead, or somebody else,

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:05.810
will still need to work with them

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.350
to look into it and to fix it.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.900
And this is a very bad attitude.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.850
You shouldn't walk around as an intern

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.350
with a mindset that
somebody else will still

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.820
check my work, that somebody else will fix

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.030
all the last errors that I didn't catch.

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.620
You should make sure that your work

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.730
is client-ready from the get-go.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.100
And one of the little tips and tricks

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.670
that I learned at McKinsey
early on from a project lead

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:28.200
is that you should always
ask yourself the question,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:30.137
whenever you submit a piece of work,

00:04:30.137 --> 00:04:33.797
"Is this ready to be sent
to a board-level member

00:04:33.797 --> 00:04:34.910
"of my client?"

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.490
So would you be comfortable
sending your piece of work

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:39.600
directly to a C-Level member

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.450
of your client organization,
with your name under it?

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:43.870
Would you be comfortable with that?

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.800
And if you would not be
comfortable with that,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.000
then probably your work isn't done yet.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.760
Then probably you are not there,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.040
where you really should be.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.020
Do the double-checks,
do the triple-checks.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.810
Make sure that your work is error-free,

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.700
that there are no bugs in your Excel model

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.770
that all the formulas
are set up correctly,

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.890
and only then submit it
to your project leader

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:02.510
as your final piece of work.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.923
The second is how good you
are with the technical skills

00:05:04.923 --> 00:05:07.835
that you will need in your
daily consulting life.

00:05:07.835 --> 00:05:10.670
And in MBB consulting, this
comes down to using Excel

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:13.370
and using PowerPoint as
the two technical tools

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:14.750
most frequently used.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.620
And of course, not all
interns are business graduates

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.670
who have already extensive
knowledge in using these tools.

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.770
Even with a liberal arts
degree, you can intern

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.280
with MBB, so McKinsey, BCG, or Bain.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.530
And of course, people will
be highly understanding

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.227
that in the beginning,
maybe your Excel skills

00:05:29.227 --> 00:05:31.260
aren't very well, because you didn't work

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:32.840
with Excel that much in the past.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.140
But what people will
expect is that you pick up

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.170
these skills quickly.

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.670
Because the reality is
if you are bad at Excel,

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.220
and if you're bad at PowerPoint,

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.390
then especially in the junior
consultant level tasks,

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:45.550
you aren't much of a help to the team.

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:47.640
So my advice here would
be that if you struggle

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:48.650
with Excel or PowerPoint,

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:50.590
make sure that you pick up the skill.

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:52.770
Maybe even invest your
weekends checking out

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.170
some other YouTube videos
or some online courses

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.590
to make sure that you build
the skill set quickly,

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.580
because it just is an important
skill set of a consultant.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.580
And of course, if you
have not seen it yet,

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.910
I have a whole playlist on this channel

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:08.920
on how to create consulting-style management presentations.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.150
I will link the playlist
here somewhere above me

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:12.130
for you to check it out.

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.077
Have a look, I believe it's
really worthwhile your time.

00:06:14.077 --> 00:06:16.240
And if you're really
serious to build these

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.060
consulting-level PowerPoint
presentation skills,

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:21.080
I even built a multi-hour course,

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.480
teaching you everything that is required

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.020
to build PowerPoint
presentations in the style

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:27.730
of MBB consulting firms.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:29.810
I have already over 10,000 students

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:31.820
taking the course, with lots of reviews.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:33.640
If you're interested, check out the link

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.580
to the course below in the description.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:36.830
Have a look through the reviews,

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:37.940
whether this might be something

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:39.580
that might add value to you as well.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.080
Regarding your analytical skills,

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.430
the last tip and advice
that I wanna give you

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.960
how you can portray, how you
can show your analytical skills

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.100
is yet your participation
in problem solving sessions.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.760
So what is a problem solving session?

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:54.170
In MBB consulting firms,
frequently you will

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:55.830
come together with your team,

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:58.030
with your project lead,
maybe with other associates

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:00.740
on the team, often also partners joining.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:02.810
And then you discuss
the important problems

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.430
that you are currently trying to solve.

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:05.830
And via the team discussion,

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:07.890
and via everybody's contribution,

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.460
you try to come up with
the best possible solution

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.010
for a problem that you are facing.

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:13.890
And this is now your opportunity to shine,

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:15.740
because these are now
team internal meetings.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:16.830
If you have a good idea,

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:18.770
if you have a good contribution to make,

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:21.540
don't be shy, and let the team
participate on your thoughts.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:22.780
Because more often than not,

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.040
you might have a good idea
on how to move forward

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:25.920
a certain aspect.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.090
And this is especially true

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.540
if you have been actually
working on a specific topic

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:30.760
in the last days or week,

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.950
because then very likely,
nobody on your team

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:35.610
will have more in-depth
knowledge of all the numbers,

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.120
of all the data that you've
been crunching than yourself.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:39.770
So don't be shy, share your thoughts.

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:42.170
Especially if it's an
internal team meeting,

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:43.400
where the risk of saying something

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.610
which might be perceived in
a negative way by a client

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:46.443
is very low.

00:07:46.443 --> 00:07:48.570
The second key dimension
that you are going to be

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.370
evaluated against are your client skills.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.410
And this basically comes
down to how you interact

00:07:54.410 --> 00:07:57.390
with the clients, and of
course, also, how the clients

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.310
perceive you as a consultant.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:00.520
So how does your project lead,

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.760
how do your other team members evaluate

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.577
whether you have proper client skills?

00:08:04.577 --> 00:08:07.650
And the main question that
they will be asking themselves

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:09.930
when they observe you
interacting with clients

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.820
is whether they can bring you to a client,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:14.220
whether they can trust
you to spend your time

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.410
at a client without any
of them being there.

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:17.390
So what this comes down to

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:19.470
is that you would now, of
course, wanna make sure

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.210
that in client meetings
you show that you can have

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:24.470
a positive contribution,
that the client does

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:25.990
take you seriously, and of course,

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:27.620
that you're not somebody
who would say things

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:29.480
which would hurt the client relationship,

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.270
or are unprofessional in any other way.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:32.760
And what I would recommend you here,

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:35.330
especially in the first days or weeks,

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.050
if you're a bit unsure whether
you should say something

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:41.030
in a specific instance,
it's usually better

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:44.230
to say rather too little,
than to say too much.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:46.250
As an intern, the people understand

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:48.690
if you maybe talk a bit
less than other colleagues

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.340
in client meetings, and actually
would be rather surprising

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.070
if in a client meeting,
you as an intern would take

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.150
the more dominant role
in the conversation.

00:08:56.150 --> 00:08:57.770
So while, of course,
you should try to avoid

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.840
to come across as shy or insecure,

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.670
usually saying a bit too little

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:03.100
is better than saying too much.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.697
The reason is that
especially as an intern,

00:09:04.697 --> 00:09:06.160
and maybe you didn't have that much

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.320
of work experience before consulting,

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:11.190
it is sometimes hard to
assess whether a certain thing

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:13.790
that maybe crosses your mind
in that specific instance

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:17.010
is now really appropriate
and helpful for the meeting.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:19.410
But of course, sitting in
all these client meetings

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.990
and saying nothing all the time

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:22.310
is not a good strategy.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:24.100
'Cause you need to show
some client skills.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:26.380
You need to show that
you are able to interact

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:27.947
in a helpful way with clients.

00:09:27.947 --> 00:09:30.050
And I already created a whole video

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:32.100
about how you as a junior colleague

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:34.470
can really add value to meetings,

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.550
even if you're not the most senior person

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:37.383
in the meeting.

00:09:37.383 --> 00:09:39.240
So I highly encourage you
to check out the video.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.040
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:43.080
There are many great tips in this video.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.910
Just one thing that I
wanna mention right here

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:47.330
is that what you can
always do also as an intern

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:48.840
to be the number guy.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.780
So as an intern, after some days or weeks,

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.440
you will likely have received some kind

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:54.730
of analysis package that
you have been working on,

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:57.510
maybe some Excel data sheets
that you have been analyzing.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:01.420
So now you are an expert on
this specific piece of data

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:03.919
on this specific package of analysis

00:10:03.919 --> 00:10:04.940
that you were asked to work on.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:06.790
So whenever the discussion touches

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:09.110
this subject field that
you've been working on,

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.830
make sure that you are really
the expert on that field.

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:13.870
So in the meeting, maybe
have your laptop with you,

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:15.270
have all the data with you,

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:16.990
so if there's any question on some number,

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:19.650
make sure that you are able to
pull the number out quickly,

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:21.930
that you can directly tell
the reliable solution.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:23.470
And this will help you to come across as

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.118
a relevant and competent
member of the consulting team

00:10:26.118 --> 00:10:27.090
towards the client.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.050
But this will also help your project lead,

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:31.090
because he now builds the confidence.

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:32.320
He now builds the trust in you,

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.330
that you have all your numbers in order,

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:36.180
that you have the big
picture of all the things

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.020
that you're doing, and
whenever he's with you,

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.130
he can trust you to know all the things

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:41.710
that you have been working on in detail.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:43.230
And this now also helps your project lead,

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.330
because now your project
lead doesn't need to prepare

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.200
all these things to
that level of detail now

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:48.197
for all the future meetings.

00:10:48.197 --> 00:10:51.420
And in the second area, how
you control your client skills

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.440
is especially after you
are a couple of more weeks

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.610
into the internship is
now to take the lead

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:57.750
for certain sections of client meetings.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.930
So of course, there are different
types of client meetings,

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:01.440
different types of interactions.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.110
But often, several topics are covered

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:04.280
in these client meetings.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:06.680
So after some time that
you feel confident enough,

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:07.890
suggest to your project lead

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:09.070
that now you will take the lead

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.040
for the parts of the meeting that cover

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.370
the specific work that
you have been working on.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.470
And often, after some time,

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:16.030
the project lead will
actually suggest this

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:17.180
to yourself as well.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:18.730
So show him that you're ready for that,

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:20.650
that you're willing and
able to take the lead,

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:22.980
leading your client through
some contents that you prepared,

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.310
or anything similar to that.

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:26.640
So to sum up, make sure
that you show your team

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:28.180
that they can trust you with clients,

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.250
that you can interact
well with the clients.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:31.390
And especially in the beginning,

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:32.250
if you're a bit in doubt,

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:35.130
probably rather don't say
something than say something.

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:36.250
But then after some time,

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:37.900
try to actively take the lead.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:39.460
Look out for these opportunities

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:40.790
where you can show your team

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:42.400
that you interact well with clients.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.080
Take the lead on some presentations.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.690
Take the lead, at least on
parts of some client meetings.

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:49.090
If you present certain
sections of a presentation,

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:50.420
and if you do all of this well,

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:52.420
then for sure you will tick all the boxes

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:54.170
for the evaluation of an intern.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:55.490
The third key skill area

00:11:55.490 --> 00:11:57.850
where you need to show your
competence as an intern

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:01.120
to secure your full-time
offer is team skills.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.340
Here you will be evaluated how
you interact with the team,

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.250
whether you are a team player or not,

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:08.960
whether you are somebody who
is slowing the team down,

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:11.580
or whether you're somebody who
makes a positive contribution

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:14.680
towards the team atmosphere,
towards the positive,

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:16.440
collaborative nature in the team room.

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.180
And this is so important,
because in consulting,

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:21.300
you will spend so much
time with your colleagues.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.260
You will sit in one team
room for days and days,

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:26.660
often even until late in the evening.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:28.210
And you just do not want to work together

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.220
with people who are annoying,
who have negative vibe,

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:33.530
who are not contributing
anything to make all of this

00:12:33.530 --> 00:12:34.630
a great experience.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.950
Of course, the more senior
you get as a consultant,

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:38.720
the more you will be evaluated against

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:40.950
how well you are to mentor
more junior colleagues,

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.550
how well you coach more junior colleagues,

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:46.210
all of this is, of course, a
bit difficult as an intern,

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:47.370
because you are literally

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.000
the most junior colleague out there.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:49.880
So what can you do?

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:52.100
How can you make an impact on your team?

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:54.430
The first suggestion I
have is to really avoid

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:57.390
sending too many negative
vibes and complaints.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:58.850
Of course, it's completely okay

00:12:58.850 --> 00:13:01.240
to have some banter with your
colleagues in the evenings,

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.453
and if there's anything that's going on

00:13:02.453 --> 00:13:04.380
that really makes you uncomfortable,

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:05.227
of course you should say that,

00:13:05.227 --> 00:13:07.400
and you should be encouraged to say that.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.120
What you should avoid is to be somebody

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.330
who just complains all the
time about all kinds of things.

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.000
Don't spread negative vibes
to the team all the time.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:16.820
This is just draining energy.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.210
Yes, sometimes working in
consulting can be tough.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.240
Sometimes the days go on for much longer

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:22.887
than many of your teammates
will have hoped for,

00:13:22.887 --> 00:13:25.360
but it really doesn't help to
talk about it all the time,

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:28.640
to always mention how bad and
annoying the situation is.

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:29.750
Instead, whenever possible,

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:31.460
try to make a positive contribution.

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:33.770
Spread positivity,
motivate your colleagues.

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:36.660
Just contribute to a positive
atmosphere in the team room.

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:38.200
This is just so important,

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.840
and I just cannot stress it enough,

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.760
also as having been a project lead,

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:44.210
how difficult it is to
work with colleagues

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:47.360
who just have this constantly
negative vibe around them.

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:49.280
The second suggestion that I have for you

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:51.620
in order for you to
portray your team skills

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:53.050
is to just be observant.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:54.310
Observe your colleagues.

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:57.290
And sometimes it will happen
that your whole team is quiet

00:13:57.290 --> 00:13:59.320
in a crunch, and as an
intern, maybe you feel

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:00.840
like that in this specific moment,

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.490
you just cannot contribute
that much to the work.

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.340
And in these situations, ask yourself,

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:08.000
what could be other things
that you can do right now

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:09.070
to help your team?

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:10.710
And these are often some little things.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:14.140
So what about you tidy up,
you clean up your team room?

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:15.137
Because maybe it's getting late,

00:14:15.137 --> 00:14:16.700
and your whole team is in a crunch

00:14:16.700 --> 00:14:19.100
to finish off this last
piece of work that they need

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:21.370
to send out in order for
them to call it a day.

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:22.880
And then what's really annoying is that

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:24.960
if then you still need
to clean up and tidy up

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.400
the team room before you can go.

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.680
So if you as an intern,
maybe have a bit more slack,

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:29.963
maybe don't have that many things to do,

00:14:29.963 --> 00:14:31.960
then just be comfortable to take up

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:32.793
this kind of work as well.

00:14:32.793 --> 00:14:34.320
And just tidy up the team room.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.350
Make sure that you get
everything ready for the team

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.180
to leave whenever they are finished.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.450
On a similar situation, if
you feel that the whole team

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:42.730
is in a crunch, and maybe
you have a bit more time,

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.760
then why don't you take the responsibility

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:46.340
to order food for the team?

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.360
Or maybe you get a
coffee from the cafeteria

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:49.193
for the team?

00:14:49.193 --> 00:14:51.940
So of course, as an intern,
it is not your prime job

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:54.250
to get the coffee, to
order the food, and so on.

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:56.130
And you will experience
it frequently enough

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.710
that your teammates will do
these same things for you.

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:00.140
But these are just the little things

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:01.640
that will be appreciated.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:02.660
People will notice that.

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:04.520
And these are also
things that will let them

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:05.960
perceive you as a team player,

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:08.420
as somebody who adds to
the whole team dynamic.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.030
And this is always a great opportunity.

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:11.550
And the last suggestion that I have

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.560
is to organize team events.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:15.060
Of course, this depends always a bit

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.890
on the culture of the consulting firm.

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:18.480
But at least in MBB consulting,

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.560
it's quite common that every month or so,

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.030
the team goes out in the
evening for a team event.

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.560
And these can be all kinds of things.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:27.310
So when I was a consultant,
we visited escape rooms.

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:29.010
We went to some sport events.

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:30.400
We had a wine tasting.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:32.320
We had an event together
with some artists,

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.140
drawing, paintings, whatever you feel like

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:35.490
could be fun for the whole team

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.530
might be fair game for such a team event.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:38.620
And often the project lead

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:40.950
asks for a consultant who
can organize the event

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:41.930
for the whole team.

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:43.510
And this, of course, from my perspective,

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:46.150
would be a perfect opportunity
for an intern to shine.

00:15:46.150 --> 00:15:47.380
By organizing a team event,

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:48.907
you can show your organizational skills,

00:15:48.907 --> 00:15:51.520
and just contribute to the
whole team having a good time.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:53.390
But of course, if you
do that, then make sure

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.800
that you can get it right,
that you can organize this

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.370
in a smooth way that really
helps the whole team.

00:15:58.370 --> 00:16:00.260
So to sum up, to show your team skills,

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:01.670
make sure that you're a team player,

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:03.320
that you spread positive vibes,

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.400
that you help your team also
on minor tasks if required,

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.350
and hey, why not organizing a team event,

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:10.060
if there's a good opportunity for that?

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:13.070
So to sum up, the three
most important skill areas

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:14.990
that you will be evaluated against

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:17.760
is your analytical skills,
your client skills,

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:19.120
and your team skills.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:21.220
So make sure that during your internship,

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:23.290
you show that you can perform well

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:25.040
against all these dimensions.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:27.690
Now, of course, I'd be super
interested to hear from you.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:30.450
What were your experiences
in your internships?

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:32.580
Were you already able
to secure a job offer

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:33.580
after an internship?

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:36.440
What were the tips and
strategies that you used?

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.760
I'd highly appreciate it if
you share your strategies

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:40.620
and insights here in the
comments section below,

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:43.630
so that other viewers of
this video can benefit

00:16:43.630 --> 00:16:44.463
from that as well.

